<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Abhira dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">203 CE – 370 CE (approx.) · capital Nashik</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Deccan dynasty that took over the Nashik region from the Satavahanas and founded the era later used by the Kalachuris.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhiras</a> were a pastoral people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, first serving as generals under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>. As the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty</a> weakened in the early 3rd century, an Abhira chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishvarasena">Ishvarasena</a>, established a kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. An era beginning in 248 or 249, perhaps founded by him, was later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalachuri-chedi-era">Kalachuri-Chedi era</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty is known mainly from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishvarasena">Ishvarasena</a> at the Buddhist caves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a>, recording an endowment by a laywoman, and from coins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> say ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhira</a> kings ruled. They seem to have controlled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and Konkan.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhira</a> kingdom declined in the 4th century; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription lists the Abhiras among peoples who submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>. Their lands passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traikutaka-dynasty">Traikutakas</a> and later to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-mahishmati">Kalachuris of Mahishmati</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 203 CE – 370 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:63.74%;width:3.32%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>203 CE – 370 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nashik</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ishvarasena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Abhiras</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=286&amp;focus=abhira-dynasty" aria-label="Abhira dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6670 1437 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Abhira dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6990 1693L7165 1820"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7165 1820h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6990 1693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6990 1693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nashik. <a href="/atlas?year=286&amp;focus=abhira-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
